\title{{[}{[}=std::interop{]}{]} An Annotation for Interoperation with
Targets}
\author{&Adrian
Johnston\\&<\href{mailto:ajohnston54637@gmail.com}{\nolinkurl{ajohnston54637@gmail.com}}>\\}
\date{unassigned}

\begin{document}
\hypertarget{title-block-header}{\label{title-block-header}}
\maketitle

\section{\texorpdfstring{Abstract\hyperref[abstract]{}}{Abstract}}\label{abstract}

This paper proposes
\VERB|\OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\AttributeTok{interop}\OperatorTok{]]}|,
a standard annotation marking a declaration for exposure to a foreign
execution environment. The annotation has no effect on the program, and
the meaning of target names is left unspecified. A reference code
generator is provided for a number of languages.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Motivation\hyperref[motivation]{}}{Motivation}}\label{motivation}

To propose a standard annotation,
\VERB|\OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\AttributeTok{interop}\OperatorTok{]]}|,
marking a declaration for exposure to a foreign execution environment,
with an optional target list. It has no effect on the program and no
target list semantics are to be specified. As context,
\href{https://wg21.link/p3394r4}{{[}P3394R4{]}} supplies the mechanism
but no vocabulary, so each library will define its own
\VERB|\NormalTok{exposed\_t}|. By adding
\VERB|\OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\AttributeTok{interop}\OperatorTok{]]}|
different libraries and tools should not need to define their own
\VERB|\NormalTok{exposed\_t}|. This attribute is proposed with the
intention of it being used as the basis for non-proliferation treaties
around interoperability annotation. Although, that is hard to control.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Proposal\hyperref[proposal]{}}{Proposal}}\label{proposal}

This proposal adds a class \VERB|\NormalTok{interop\_type}| and a
constant \VERB|\NormalTok{interop}| of that type to a new header,
\VERB|\OperatorTok{\textless{}}\NormalTok{interop}\OperatorTok{\textgreater{}}|.
The \VERB|\NormalTok{interop}| constant and the result of calling it
with \VERB|\KeywordTok{operator}\OperatorTok{()}| are required to be
valid annotation operands under
\href{https://wg21.link/p3394r4}{{[}P3394R4{]}}. The call operator
accepts any number of arguments convertible to
\VERB|\NormalTok{string\_view}| and returns a new
\VERB|\NormalTok{interop\_type}| holding the targets in static storage
and in call order. \VERB|\NormalTok{targets}\OperatorTok{()}| returns
that list unmodified, duplicates included, and empty for
\VERB|\NormalTok{interop}| itself. Embedded nulls in a string literal
argument are preserved. Whether equal strings in the list share a
pointer is unspecified.

The proposed interface is as follows, however the implementation is for
exposition only.

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\KeywordTok{namespace}\NormalTok{ std }\OperatorTok{\{}
  \KeywordTok{struct}\NormalTok{ interop\_type }\OperatorTok{\{}
    \KeywordTok{template}\OperatorTok{\textless{}}\NormalTok{convertible\_to}\OperatorTok{\textless{}}\NormalTok{string\_view}\OperatorTok{\textgreater{}...}\NormalTok{ S}\OperatorTok{\textgreater{}}
    \KeywordTok{consteval}\NormalTok{ interop\_type }\KeywordTok{operator}\OperatorTok{()(}\KeywordTok{const}\NormalTok{ S}\OperatorTok{\&...}\NormalTok{ s}\OperatorTok{)} \KeywordTok{const} \OperatorTok{\{}
\NormalTok{      vector}\OperatorTok{\textless{}}\KeywordTok{const} \DataTypeTok{char}\OperatorTok{*\textgreater{}}\NormalTok{ list}\OperatorTok{(}\NormalTok{list\_}\OperatorTok{,}\NormalTok{ list\_ }\OperatorTok{+}\NormalTok{ size\_}\OperatorTok{);}
      \OperatorTok{(}\NormalTok{list}\OperatorTok{.}\NormalTok{push\_back}\OperatorTok{(}\NormalTok{define\_static\_string}\OperatorTok{(}\NormalTok{whole\_}\OperatorTok{(}\NormalTok{s}\OperatorTok{))),} \OperatorTok{...);}
      \KeywordTok{auto}\NormalTok{ kept }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ define\_static\_array}\OperatorTok{(}\NormalTok{list}\OperatorTok{);}
      \ControlFlowTok{return} \OperatorTok{\{}\NormalTok{kept}\OperatorTok{.}\NormalTok{data}\OperatorTok{(),}\NormalTok{ kept}\OperatorTok{.}\NormalTok{size}\OperatorTok{()\};}
    \OperatorTok{\}}
    \KeywordTok{constexpr}\NormalTok{ span}\OperatorTok{\textless{}}\KeywordTok{const} \DataTypeTok{char}\OperatorTok{*} \KeywordTok{const}\OperatorTok{\textgreater{}}\NormalTok{ targets}\OperatorTok{(}\DataTypeTok{void}\OperatorTok{)} \KeywordTok{const} \OperatorTok{\{}
      \ControlFlowTok{return} \OperatorTok{\{}\NormalTok{list\_}\OperatorTok{,}\NormalTok{ size\_}\OperatorTok{\};}
    \OperatorTok{\}}
    \KeywordTok{template}\OperatorTok{\textless{}}\KeywordTok{class}\NormalTok{ S}\OperatorTok{\textgreater{}}
    \KeywordTok{static} \KeywordTok{consteval}\NormalTok{ string\_view whole\_}\OperatorTok{(}\KeywordTok{const}\NormalTok{ S}\OperatorTok{\&}\NormalTok{ s}\OperatorTok{)} \OperatorTok{\{} \CommentTok{// exposition only}
      \ControlFlowTok{if} \KeywordTok{constexpr} \OperatorTok{(}\NormalTok{is\_array\_v}\OperatorTok{\textless{}}\NormalTok{S}\OperatorTok{\textgreater{})} \ControlFlowTok{return} \OperatorTok{\{}\NormalTok{s}\OperatorTok{,}\NormalTok{ extent\_v}\OperatorTok{\textless{}}\NormalTok{S}\OperatorTok{\textgreater{}} \OperatorTok{{-}} \DecValTok{1}\OperatorTok{\};}
      \ControlFlowTok{else} \ControlFlowTok{return}\NormalTok{ s}\OperatorTok{;}
    \OperatorTok{\}}
    \KeywordTok{const} \DataTypeTok{char}\OperatorTok{*} \KeywordTok{const}\OperatorTok{*}\NormalTok{ list\_}\OperatorTok{;}                         \CommentTok{// exposition only}
    \DataTypeTok{size\_t}\NormalTok{ size\_}\OperatorTok{;}                                     \CommentTok{// exposition only}
  \OperatorTok{\};}
  \KeywordTok{inline} \KeywordTok{constexpr}\NormalTok{ interop\_type interop}\OperatorTok{\{\};}
\OperatorTok{\}}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

The annotation may appear wherever
\href{https://wg21.link/p3394r4}{{[}P3394R4{]}} allows one.
\VERB|\OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\AttributeTok{interop}\OperatorTok{]]}|
marks a declaration for exposure without naming a target, and
\VERB|\OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\AttributeTok{interop}\OperatorTok{(}\StringTok{"ECMAScript"}\OperatorTok{,}\AttributeTok{ }\StringTok{"Python"}\OperatorTok{)]]}|
names a target list. A target list can also be built up using
\VERB|\NormalTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\NormalTok{interop}| and reused as
is the \VERB|\OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{script}\OperatorTok{]]}|
attribute below. It is unspecified if annotating a class or namespace
indicates anything about its members.

What follows is an example involving a thermostat.

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\KeywordTok{inline} \KeywordTok{constexpr} \KeywordTok{auto}\NormalTok{ script }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ std}\OperatorTok{::}\NormalTok{interop}\OperatorTok{(}\StringTok{"ECMAScript"}\OperatorTok{,} \StringTok{"Python"}\OperatorTok{);}

\KeywordTok{class} \OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\AttributeTok{interop}\OperatorTok{]]}\NormalTok{ thermostat\_type }\OperatorTok{\{}
\KeywordTok{public}\OperatorTok{:}
  \OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\AttributeTok{interop}\OperatorTok{]]} \OperatorTok{\textasciitilde{}}\NormalTok{thermostat\_type}\OperatorTok{(}\DataTypeTok{void}\OperatorTok{)} \OperatorTok{=} \ControlFlowTok{default}\OperatorTok{;}

  \OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\AttributeTok{interop}\OperatorTok{]]} \DataTypeTok{double}\NormalTok{ current\_temp}\OperatorTok{(}\DataTypeTok{void}\OperatorTok{)} \KeywordTok{const}\OperatorTok{;}
  \OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\AttributeTok{interop}\OperatorTok{]]} \DataTypeTok{double}\NormalTok{ temp\_setting}\OperatorTok{(}\DataTypeTok{void}\OperatorTok{)} \KeywordTok{const}\OperatorTok{;}

  \OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{script}\OperatorTok{]]} \DataTypeTok{bool}\NormalTok{ furnace\_on}\OperatorTok{(}\DataTypeTok{void}\OperatorTok{)} \KeywordTok{const}\OperatorTok{;}
  \OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{script}\OperatorTok{]]} \DataTypeTok{bool}\NormalTok{ fan\_on}\OperatorTok{(}\DataTypeTok{void}\OperatorTok{)} \KeywordTok{const}\OperatorTok{;}

  \OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\AttributeTok{interop}\OperatorTok{(}\StringTok{"Rust"}\OperatorTok{)]]} \DataTypeTok{void}\NormalTok{ set\_temp\_setting}\OperatorTok{(}\DataTypeTok{double}\NormalTok{ t\_}\OperatorTok{);}

\KeywordTok{private}\OperatorTok{:}
  \CommentTok{// ...}
\OperatorTok{\};}

\OperatorTok{[[=}\AttributeTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\AttributeTok{interop}\OperatorTok{]]}\NormalTok{ thermostat\_type thermostat\_factory}\OperatorTok{(}\DataTypeTok{double}\NormalTok{ current}\OperatorTok{,}
                                                     \DataTypeTok{double}\NormalTok{ setting}\OperatorTok{);}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

No change to the existing standard is proposed beyond the inclusion of
\VERB|\OperatorTok{\textless{}}\NormalTok{interop}\OperatorTok{\textgreater{}}|,
\VERB|\NormalTok{interop\_type}| and \VERB|\NormalTok{interop}|.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Reference
Implementation\hyperref[reference-implementation]{}}{Reference Implementation}}\label{reference-implementation}

In order to validate the design, the above code was used to generate a C
header and to generate bindings for C, ECMAScript, Python and Rust.

Here is the
\href{https://whatchamacallem.github.io/std_interop/}{website} for the
outputs. It provides an index of the files generated by the
\href{https://github.com/whatchamacallem/std_interop}{repository}.

The code generator is
\href{https://whatchamacallem.github.io/std_interop/interop.cpp.html}{\VERB|\NormalTok{interop}\OperatorTok{.}\NormalTok{cpp}|}
and it emits 4 bindings and a C header in under 400 LOC. Everything
passes tests and is formatted correctly.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Design
Decisions\hyperref[design-decisions]{}}{Design Decisions}}\label{design-decisions}

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  Providing a naming system for foreign languages in the C++ standard is
  recognized as unmaintainable. Other standards are welcome to make
  recommendations for their own names.
\item
  The reference implementation of \VERB|\NormalTok{interop\_type}| uses
  \VERB|\NormalTok{define\_static\_string}| and
  \VERB|\NormalTok{define\_static\_array}| from
  \href{https://wg21.link/p3491r3}{{[}P3491R3{]}}.
\end{itemize}

\section{\texorpdfstring{Future
Work\hyperref[future-work]{}}{Future Work}}\label{future-work}

Any modifications to the standard will be described in detail in later
revisions of this proposal.

\href{https://wg21.link/p3394r4}{{[}P3394R4{]}} permits annotations on
namespaces, but the meaning of
\VERB|\NormalTok{std}\OperatorTok{::}\NormalTok{interop}| on a namespace
for its members is left open. Discussion is needed with independent
interoperability project maintainers once this proposal is concrete.

